      "text": "Given the equations of the two lines:\n\\[ y = mx + 4 \\]\n\\[ y = 3x + b \\]\n\nWe know that these lines intersect at the point \\((6, 10)\\). Therefore, we can substitute \\(x = 6\\) and \\(y = 10\\) into both equations to find the values of \\(m\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\((6, 10)\\) into the equation \\(y = mx + 4\\):\n\\[ 10 = m(6) + 4 \\]\n\\[ 10 = 6m + 4 \\]\n\\[ 10 - 4 = 6m \\]\n\\[ 6 = 6m \\]\n\\[ m = 1 \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\((6, 10)\\) into the equation \\(y = 3x + b\\):\n\\[ 10 = 3(6) + b \\]\n\\[ 10 = 18 + b \\]\n\\[ 10 - 18 = b \\]\n\\[ b = -8 \\]\n\nNow that we have \\(m = 1\\) and \\(b = -8\\), we can find the value of \\(b + m\\):\n\\[ b + m = -8 + 1 = -7 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(b + m\\) is \\(\\boxed{-7}\\).",
