      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the value of \\( b + m \\), we start by using the given information that the lines intersect at the point \\((6, 10)\\). This means that when \\( x = 6 \\), the \\( y \\)-values of both lines are equal to 10. We will use this information to set up and solve two equations.\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equation of the first line \\( y = mx + 4 \\):\n\\[\n10 = m(6) + 4\n\\]\nSimplify and solve for \\( m \\):\n\\[\n10 = 6m + 4\n\\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[\n6 = 6m\n\\]\nDivide both sides by 6:\n\\[\nm = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equation of the second line \\( y = 3x + b \\):\n\\[\n10 = 3(6) + b\n\\]\nSimplify and solve for \\( b \\):\n\\[\n10 = 18 + b\n\\]\nSubtract 18 from both sides:\n\\[\n-8 = b\n\\]\nSo, we have \\( b = -8 \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the value of \\( b + m \\):\n\\[\nb + m = -8 + 1 = -7\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( b + m \\) is \\(\\boxed{-7}\\).",
